Editor&rsquo;s Note


Amid heightened global economic uncertainty driven by the Trump administration&rsquo;s high tariffs and rising tensions between Israel and Iran placing the Middle East on a knife&rsquo;s edge, the Association of Southeast Asian Nations (ASEAN) has joined hands with the Gulf Cooperation Council (GCC) and China. Musa Damao, Secretary-General of the Council for Peace and Justice Advocacy (Bangsa Moro), interprets this cooperation as part of a broader movement by the Global South to assert greater autonomy and balance beyond traditional East-West dynamics. However, he also suggests that ASEAN is likely to maintain a neutral stance regarding the situation in the Middle East. Secretary-General Damao wrote this column prior to the ceasefire between Israel and Iran.



&nbsp;


<img alt="Musa Damao, peace and development advocate in the Philippines especially in the Bangsamoro Autonomous Region in Muslim Mindanao(BARMM), Executive Director of Bangsamoro Dialogue for Peace and Justice, Inc., part-time professor of Notre Dame of Midsayap College, writer of The Lobbyist. ⓒ천지일보" src="https://www.newscj.com/news/photo/202507/3290035_3359374_3543.jpg" />
Musa Damao, peace and development advocate in the Philippines especially in the Bangsamoro Autonomous Region in Muslim Mindanao(BARMM), Executive Director of Bangsamoro Dialogue for Peace and Justice, Inc., part-time professor of Notre Dame of Midsayap College, writer of The Lobbyist. ⓒ천지일보



The Southeast Asia region has been considered part of the Global South, mostly dependent to western products and services. The U.S. is seen as the molder of the regional order. The U.S. companies and corporations abound in the region, making it an important market of U.S. More than 6,200 U.S. companies operate within ASEAN, and in 2020, 630,000 U.S. travelers spent more than $1.3 billion in Southeast Asia. The U.S. goods and services trade with Association of South East Asian Nation (ASEAN) are estimated to be more or less $ 441.7 billion in 2021.&nbsp;&nbsp;

ASEAN is an organization in Southeast Asia, comprising ten countries working together for economic development, political stability, and security cooperation within the region. It was established in 1967, by the founding countries namely, Thailand, Singapore, the Philippines, Malaysia, and Indonesia. Later, Vietnam, Myanmar, Laos, Cambodia, and Brunei joined the association.&nbsp;

In addition, ASEAN focuses on political and economic growth, social progress, the evolution of socio-cultural relations, and strategies to address emerging regional challenges.

GCC and the ASEAN

The Gulf Cooperation Council (GCC) is a regional economic and political union of the Arab States in West Asia. GCC holds the 30% percent of the global oil production, which is a vital region for the emerging economies, offering key opportunities for the ASEAN to improve their trade relations. The relationship of the two blocs goes beyond oil as it includes strategic sectors such as food, financial services, technology (AI) and even tourism. Digitalization is also an avenue for cooperation and collaboration.

Recently, the GCC and ASEAN Plus China Trilateral Summit agreed to &ldquo;chart a unified and collective path towards a peaceful, prosperous, and just future.&rdquo;

The Trilateral Summit was concluded by reaffirming their collective resolve to work hand in hand to unleash the full potential of their partnership, and ensure that their cooperation translates into tangible benefits for their peoples and societies.

This GCC-ASEAN alliance holds an immense value for the people of Asia especially West Asia and Southeast Asia as it redefines international trade dynamics beyond the confine of East-west lines, unveiling the pivotal moment for the Global South to rise above Western biases. This is part and parcel of the movement of history, providing a venue for economic opportunity and geopolitical balance.

In 2023, bilateral trade between the two emerging economic power blocs reached over $100 billion including the major products such as petrochemicals, electronics, machinery and even processed foods.&nbsp;

China, the U.S. and the ASEAN

During a high-level conference in Tehran, the Iranian Ambassador to ASEAN Mohammad Boroujerdi lauded ASEAN vision headed by Malaysia, stating, &ldquo;Iran sees great potential in partnerships with ASEAN, and Malaysia&rsquo;s visionary leadership will strengthen ties between our regions. The theme of inclusivity and sustainability aligns perfectly with our shared goals.&rdquo; The Islamic Republic of Iran has deep-rooted socio-cultural connection with the ASEAN.&nbsp;

Undeniably, ASEAN has also a vital diplomatic relation with Israel. Israel and ASEAN have economic ties involving millions of dollars. Each country holds a bilateral cooperation, holding mutual economic benefits with Israel. Also, the State of Israel is the most important ally of the U.S., which in turn, will indirectly influence the stance of ASEAN in the present regional conflict. The U.S. is at the back of Israel since its founding as they shared democratic values.&nbsp;

Malaysia, as the head of the ASEAN, is presently an open critic of the Zionist State especially since the beginning of the Gaza Genocide. Prime Minister Anwar Ebrahim has been a vocal supporter of the Palestinians. Malaysia, Indonesia and Brunei might side Iran while the rest of the member-states will be for Israel. But a great possibility is that ASEAN will form a neutral posture, employing a balancing act between the U.S.-backed Israel and China-backed Iran.&nbsp;

As the old order crumbles, the GCC and ASEAN might force themselves to forge new trading links to others for the betterment of their respective regions.&nbsp;

Joanne Lin, senior fellow and co-coordinator of the ASEAN Studies Centre at the ISEAS&ndash;Yusof Ishak Institute, in the think tank&rsquo;s publication Fulcrum ahead of the ASEAN-GCC-China summit, wrote, &ldquo;The declining influence of the U.S. in the Global South, coupled with increasingly polarizing U.S. policies in West Asia, has opened space for alternative South-South alignments.&rdquo;

China and Russia have established a new multipolar world order, challenging the U.S, which continuously treats the world as monolithic and unipolar, reordering the global system, and constructing a world free from unilateral hegemony.&nbsp;

That attack in Iran endangers the multipolar vision of Russia, China and Iran among others. The U.S.-Israeli orchestrated assault to Iran is a message to Russia and China, that global order is still in the U.S.&rsquo;s playbook. The longer the war between Israel and Iran, the greater the possibility of Russia and China joining the war as it destroys what they invested for quite a long time.&nbsp;

Implications of Israeli-Iranian War

The entry of Russia and China to the Israeli-Iranian war may put the world into the point of no turn. China is a neighbor of ASEAN, which will ultimately feel the pain of war. Worse, in case the U.S. will fully participate in the war, China will be forced to counter it. The ASEAN will experience crises and challenges that eclipse the Japanese invasion of Southeast Asia.&nbsp;

Although the cooperation of the three economic power blocs ensures positive results, in the long-term perspective, GCC-ASEAN-China trilateral collaboration for economic stability in the Southeast Asia will be disrupted.&nbsp;

The emerging economies of ASEAN, fueled by China&rsquo;s assistance and GCC&rsquo;s investment, have been creating healthy economic environment. Unfortunately, they will face mounting challenges.&nbsp;

In economic standpoint, when Iran is forced to close the strait of Hormuz, its global ramification is inevitable. The price of oil will surge, leading to high inflation. The price of oil affects all the prices of the basic goods and commodities. This will lead to an unprecedented crisis not only in countries directly involved in war but also the entire globe.&nbsp;

The war will have far-reaching consequences in the GCC which will affect their assistance and investment in the ASEAN. Billions of dollars will go to waste.&nbsp;

Moreover, Thailand, Indonesia and Cambodia relied heavily on oil from West Asia to fuel their growing economy and transportation industry. Any disruption of oil market results in energy cost, high inflation and will stagnate their economic growth. Even Malaysia and Brunei, are not safe as rising oil prices could potentially help stoke inflation.

